turns.text:
Connection drops during BEGIN, the query, or COMMIT—all three paths have the same problem.

The catch block tries to ROLLBACK on a dead connection, which throws. That error isn't caught, so it bubbles up and masks the original error. The finally block releases the dead client back to the pool, which keeps it unusable.

More subtle: if the connection dies *after* COMMIT succeeds but before we return, the transaction actually committed to the database. But the catch block runs anyway (the error is from returning the result, not from COMMIT), so the code thinks it rolled back—the caller gets an error, retries, and applies the change twice.

The fix is to catch errors in the catch block itself, or check connection state before ROLLBACK:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // connection is dead, skip rollback
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This ensures the original error reaches the caller, and we don't crash on ROLLBACK if the connection is already gone.

