runs.text:
No — raising the limit won't fix this. Look at the log: idle stays at 0 and waiting climbs monotonically the whole hour (7→11→14→19→23→28) with no recovery in between. That's a leak, not a capacity shortfall; a real capacity problem would show idle oscillating, not pinned at zero.

The leak is in `db.js`:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  const result = await fn(client);
  client.release();
  return result;
}
```

`client.release()` only runs if `fn(client)` succeeds. If it throws, the client is checked out forever and never returned to the pool. Wrap it in try/finally:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Fix that first — raising `max` would just let the pool leak up to a higher ceiling before hitting the same wall.

