turns.text:
The connection drop gets caught, but the error handling has a flaw.

When the pool connection drops during a query, that query throws an error. The catch block tries `ROLLBACK`, but if the connection is already gone, `ROLLBACK` itself throws. That new error overwrites the original one and gets rethrown—the route handler doesn't catch it, so Express returns 500. The database automatically rolls back the transaction when the connection dies, so data is safe, but the error context is lost.

The fix is to handle ROLLBACK failure separately:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // connection already closed, rollback is implicit
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This swallows the ROLLBACK failure and rethrows the original error, preserving what actually went wrong.

